# RTK — Always Prefix Shell Commands

**Golden rule:** Every shell command MUST start with `rtk`. RTK is always safe — dedicated filters compress output; everything else passes through unchanged.

## Command Chains

Even with `&&`, prefix each command:

```bash
# ❌ Wrong
git add . && git commit -m "msg" && git push

# ✅ Correct
rtk git add . && rtk git commit -m "msg" && rtk git push
```

## Common Commands

| Workflow | Command |
|----------|---------|
| Git | `rtk git status`, `rtk git diff`, `rtk git log`, `rtk git add`, `rtk git commit`, `rtk git push` |
| Build | `rtk tsc`, `rtk lint`, `rtk npm run build`, `rtk vite build` |
| Test | `rtk vitest`, `rtk jest`, `rtk pytest`, `rtk npm test` |
| Search | `rtk grep <pattern>`, `rtk find <pattern>`, `rtk ls <path>` |
| Debug | `rtk err <cmd>`, `rtk gain`, `rtk gain --history` |

## Passthrough

Git subcommands not listed explicitly still work: `rtk git fetch`, `rtk git branch`, etc.

Use `rtk proxy <cmd>` only when you need unfiltered output for debugging.

## Do Not Skip

- Do not run bare `git`, `npm`, `npx`, `tsc`, `curl`, `docker`, `kubectl` when `rtk` has a wrapper
- Agent shell tool calls in this project always use the `rtk` prefix
